from monday hospitality will be able to  open  indoors the rest of our tourism industry  will be able to  reopen uh indoor events  will begin again for a limited number of  people  cinemas theaters concert halls will  begin the journey to  resuming and because the number of  people suffering from coronavirus in  wales continues to  go down then we're able to slightly  increase the number of people  able to be together in a purple  restaurant or cafe  from four as we'd originally anticipated  to six  and how rigidly will you stick to your  lockdown  exit plan bearing in mind the indian  variant  of coronavirus how how much will you be  monitoring that and how much will it  affect  your plans well it's already affected  our plans in the sense that we had  considered some further easements as  from  monday and have decided to hold back on  those for the moment  until we see the advice from sage which  met yesterday on our own scientific  advisors  which we expect imminently there were 17  cases of the indian variant in wales  yesterday but over 700 in england  and there's a concentration of those in  the northwest of england and  the border between the northeast of  wales and the northwest of england is  very porous  so we've decided just to pause wait to  see  just how significant the spread of the  indian variant is  and then we'll continue to take  decisions in the careful step-by-step  way we have throughout the pandemic  not wanting to put at risk the very good  position we are  in in wales today the lowest levels of  coronavirus anywhere in the united  kingdom  and the highest levels of vaccination uh  you said that you've held back  on easing various restrictions  specifically what what did you have in  mind  and what impact has the indian variant  had on you  uh well we had thought of moving ahead  with the reopening of  smaller events we'll pause that for a  moment  we were thinking of liberalizing the  rules in the way in which people  can uh meet together not just in their  extended household but beyond that we'll  pause that for the moment if the advice  on the indian variant  is uh that it is safe to move ahead we  won't need to wait for  the end of our next three week cycle to  do those things  but the indian variant is giving us  pause for  concern we don't know enough about  whether it is more transmissible than  the kent variant  we don't know enough about whether the  vaccination program  is as effective in dealing with it as it  is with other variants we have  in wales and until we're a bit clearer  on that  i think it is sensible to take a  precautionary approach  wait for the evidence and then make the  decisions  when we have a better sense of what we  might be up against  the uh welsh vaccination has been the  most successful in  in the uk if uh levels of  coronavirus increase across the border  will you then look at restrictions on  travel  not expecting to do that uh at the  moment  uh i i've heard uh you know english  ministers saying that they may  think of amending their vaccination  schedule we would look at that  too but we're in the fortunate position  in wales that in terms of second doses  over 90 percent of all 70 year olds  all 80 year olds all clear home  residents have already received the  second dose of the vaccine in wales and  we've got pretty good second dose  numbers of people  further down the age ranges as well so  we'll be keeping the vaccination program  under review in case we need to modify  it  in the face of the indian variant but  we'll be doing so  from a pretty successful platform  uh now in terms of uh domestic and  political reshuffling you've had a  reshuffle of your cabinet and you've  moved  vaughn getting away from health minister  um why was that and it's a bit of odd  timing considering we are still in the  pandemic and by all accounts  he was doing a great job uh well he said  he was doing a  very good job of course you must  remember that in wales we're a small  government  and a small cabinet so vaughn has not  disappeared at all he's  gone to take on the very significant  responsibilities  of helping us to rebuild our economy as  we come out of coronavirus  but whenever our cabinet will be  discussing uh  the impact of uh the pandemic he'll be  there with all the experience he's had  and all the advice he will be able to  bring  we're fortunate that our new health  minister elenid morgan  was already in our cabinet with  significant health responsibilities  so she comes to that job very well  prepared  uh our hope is of course that we are  putting coronavirus behind us  and the focus of my new cabinet  will be on rebuilding wales rebuilding  our health service rebuilding our  economy  dealing with that other great crisis of  our time in climate change  as we move beyond the crisis that has  gripped us for the last 15 months  uh you've repeatedly said that you'd  step down as first minister and uh  labour leader in this uh senate term is  that something you're still  planning on doing uh well my focus is on  making sure that we complete the job  uh of getting wales beyond coronavirus  and then setting us on that  ambitious path which my party set out in  our election manifesto  uh and the time will come before the end  of the seventh term when it will be  right to hand over to somebody  will be here beyond the next five years  uh but all my energies and all my  efforts are focused not on that  uh but on what happens over the next two  or three years  crucial years for wales putting the  pandemic behind us  putting us on the path to the future  creating that  agreement from wales mark drakeford fest  minister of welsh thank you very much  for speaking to us this morning  thank you
 

